==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|tempestuous}}, {{m|enm|tempestious}}, variants of {{m|enm|tempestous}}, from {{der|en|fro|tempesteus}}, {{m|fro|tempestos}}, {{m|fro|tempestuose}}, from {{der|en|la|tempestuōsus}}, equivalent to {{suffix|en|tempest|uous}}.

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Of, or resembling, a [[tempest]]; [[stormy]], [[tumultuous]].
#: {{synonyms|en|blustery|windy}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|act=I|scene=i|passage=[On a ship at sea]: a '''tempestuous''' noise of thunder and lightening heard.}}
#* {{RQ:Bentley Confutation of Atheism|sermon=World 3|pages=25–26|pageref=25|passage=[T]he Months of ''March'' and ''September'', the tvvo Æquinoxes of Our year, are the moſt vvindy and '''tempeſtuous''', the moſt unſettled and unequable of Seaſons in moſt Countries of the VVorld.}}
#* {{RQ:Doyle Return of Sherlock Holmes|story=The Adventure of the Golden Pince-Nez|passage=It was a wild, '''tempestuous''' night towards the close of November. {{...}} Outside the wind howled down Baker Street, while the rain beat fiercely against the windows. It was strange there in the very depths of the town, with ten miles of man's handiwork on every side of us, to feel the iron grip of Nature, and to be conscious that to the huge elemental forces all London was no more than the molehills that dot the fields.}}

